  "signals": [
    {
      "phrase": "into groups",
      "meaning": "Words that belong together need a shared place. A defaultdict(list) keeps one list per key and starts an empty one the first time a key shows up.",
      "pointsTo": "toolkit:defaultdict_list"
    },
    {
      "phrase": "the same letters the same number of times",
      "meaning": "Anagrams differ only in order. Anything that ignores order, like the letter counts or the letters in sorted order, is the same for every word in a group, so it can be that group's key in a hash map.",
      "pointsTo": "hashing"
    },
    {
      "phrase": "in any order",
      "meaning": "Since the order of the letters doesn't matter, put them in one fixed order: sorted() spells all the anagrams of a word the same way.",
      "pointsTo": "toolkit:sorted_key"
    },
    {
      "phrase": "lowercase English letters",
      "meaning": "Only 26 letters, so a word's letter counts fit in a list of 26 numbers, and ord() turns each letter into its slot, 0 for a up to 25 for z. As a tuple, that list is another key every anagram shares.",
      "pointsTo": "toolkit:ord_chr"
    }
  ],
  "constraintReading": "len(strs) can reach 10⁴, so comparing every pair of words means about 5·10⁷ anagram checks, each reading up to 100 letters: O(n²), far too slow. A word has at most 100 letters, so turning one word into a key, by sorting or counting its letters, costs a bounded amount of work. One pass over the n words is then O(n) time, with O(n) space for the groups.",
  "hints": {
    "clarify": "Can a word be in a group by itself? Where does a repeated word go? A word with no anagram gets a group of its own, and every copy of a repeated word goes in the same group. Try [\"ten\", \"net\", \"nett\", \"ten\"] by hand: \"ten\" and \"net\" each have one t, one e and one n, so they're anagrams, and the second \"ten\" joins them. \"nett\" has two t's, so it matches none of them: [[\"ten\", \"net\", \"ten\"], [\"nett\"]].",
    "approach": "Hash map and counting. Anagrams look different, but they share something that ignores order: their letters in sorted order. \"ten\" and \"net\" both sort to \"ent\". Use that as a dict key. Walk through `strs` once, build each word's key, and add the word to the list stored under that key. Words with the same key are anagrams, so each list is one group, and the dict's values are the answer. A tuple of the word's 26 letter counts works as a key too.",
    "whyNot": "Comparing each word with every other word, or with one word from each group so far, works, but with 10⁴ words that's up to about 5·10⁷ anagram checks: O(n²). Sorting the whole list by key, so anagrams sit side by side, works too, but that sort costs O(n log n). Watch out for keys that look safe but aren't: the set of letters (\"ten\" and \"nett\" have the same set) or the sum of the letters' codes (\"ad\" and \"bc\" add up the same).",
    "slots": {
      "setup": "A dict `groups` that starts an empty list for every new key: a `defaultdict` of lists. The key is a word's letters in sorted order; the value is the list of words that share it.",
      "loop": "Visit each word in `strs` once.",
      "update": "Add the word to the end of the list under its key. The first word with a new key starts a new group.",
      "record": "Build the word's key: sort its letters and join them back into a string. Look that key up in `groups`. Anagrams build the same key, so they find the same list.",
      "return": "Return the lists stored in `groups`, one per key. Every word is in exactly one of them."
    }
  },
  "solution": {
    "code": "class Solution:\n    def groupAnagrams(self, strs: list[str]) -> list[list[str]]:\n        groups = defaultdict(list)  # viz:setup\n        for word in strs:\n            key = \"\".join(sorted(word))  # viz:sort\n            groups[key].append(word)  # viz:add\n        return list(groups.values())  # viz:done\n",
    "explanation": "The loop visits each of the n words once. Sorting a word of k letters takes O(k log k) time, and k is at most 100, so building a key costs a bounded amount of work, and the dict lookup and the append take O(1) on average: O(n) time in total. The dict holds every word once, plus one key per group, so the space is O(n). A key of 26 letter counts works the same way: add 1 at index `ord(c) - ord(\"a\")` for each letter `c`, then turn the list into a tuple, since a list can't be a dict key. That costs O(k) per word instead of O(k log k), which only matters when words are long.",
    "toolkit": ["defaultdict_list", "sorted_key", "join"]
  },
